WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.100
Serverless platforms make scaling easier, and that is exactly why runtime controls matter.

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:14.120
Functions have limits around timeout, memory, payload size, execution duration, concurrency, and retry behavior.

3
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:19.980
Those settings affect reliability, cost, security, and downstream safety.

4
00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:23.680
A timeout should match the workflow and its dependencies.

5
00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:27.460
Memory and CPU settings affect performance and failure behavior.

6
00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:35.960
Concurrency controls can protect databases, APIs, queues, rate-limited services, and third-party providers from sudden bursts.

7
00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.760
Retries are useful only when the handler is designed for them.

8
00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.580
Async events can be delivered more than once.

9
00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:44.440
Messages can be replayed.

10
00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:48.060
A downstream failure can leave partial work completed.

11
00:00:48.160 --> 00:01:00.320
If the function retries a payment, sends duplicate emails, repeats a delete, or advances the same workflow twice, the incident may look like a business logic failure rather than an infrastructure issue.

12
00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.660
Duplicate events should be expected, not treated as rare surprises.

13
00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:08.700
Idempotency is the practical answer for risky operations.

14
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:19.920
Use idempotency keys, deduplication records, state checks, conditional writes, and safe update patterns so repeating the same event does not repeat the business effect.

15
00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:23.220
The exact design depends on the workflow.

16
00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:27.560
A notification function may tolerate duplicates differently than a billing function.

17
00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:31.340
A cleanup job may need careful state checks before deleting anything.

18
00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.640
The goal is to make retry behavior predictable.

19
00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:41.160
Dead-letter queues, failure destinations, alerts, and runbooks make failures visible and recoverable.

20
00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:48.940
Without them, a function can fail silently or retry until it creates cost, data quality, or availability problems.

21
00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:56.960
Cost abuse and resource exhaustion should be treated as serverless security concerns because fast scaling can amplify mistakes quickly.

22
00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:02.900
Bounded execution, visible failure, and downstream protection are part of secure serverless design.
